{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Recipe",
  "name": "San Marzano tomato pizza",
  "text": "San Marzano tomato pizza\n\nPreparation time: overnight\n\nCooking time: less than 10 mins\n\nYield: Makes 4 pizzas\n\nIngredients\n\nFor the pizza dough\n\n800 g '00' flour,\n\n200 g semolina flour,\n\n1 tsp salt,\n\n1 tbsp caster sugar,\n\n14g dried yeast,\n\n4 tbsp extra virgin olive oil,\n\n650 ml water,\n\nFor the topping\n\n2 x 400g tins San Marzano tomatoes,\n\n2 large cow’s milk mozzarella balls (grated),\n\n3 tbsp olive oil,\n\n3 tbsp peanut oil,\n\nsea salt and freshly ground black pepper,\n\n1 large handful basil leaves.\n\nPreparation method\n\n1. To make the dough, place the flours, salt, sugar and yeast into a large bowl and stir. Make a well in the centre of the flour and pour in the water, gradually mixing in the flour to form a soft dough.\n\n2. Tip out and knead on an oiled work surface for about 10 minutes or until the dough is smooth and elastic. Place it into a bowl, cover and leave to rise for 24 hours.\n\n3. Preheat the oven to its highest temperature. Place a heavy baking tray or pizza stone in the oven and allow it to heat.\n\n4. Remove the dough from the bowl and knead for a couple of minutes, then divide into four pieces. Allow to rest for five minutes, then stretch each piece over your hands to form a disc about 5mm/¼in thick.\n\n5. Place the circles of dough onto upturned floured baking trays or a pizza peel if you have one.\n\n6. Place the tinned tomatoes into a food processor and blend to a purée. Spoon the tomato purée thinly over the pizzas, scatter over the grated mozzarella, then drizzle over the oils. Season with sea salt and freshly ground black pepper.\n\n7. Slide each pizza off the baking tray directly onto the heated baking tray or pizza stone in the oven and cook for 5 -10 minutes, or until golden-brown and crisp.\n\n8. To serve, scatter the basil leaves over the pizza and drizzle with a little more peanut oil.",
  "recipeYield": "Makes 4 pizzas",
  "recipeIngredient": [
    "For the pizza dough: 800 g '00' flour,",
    "For the pizza dough: 200 g semolina flour,",
    "For the pizza dough: 1 tsp salt,",
    "For the pizza dough: 1 tbsp caster sugar,",
    "For the pizza dough: 14g dried yeast,",
    "For the pizza dough: 4 tbsp extra virgin olive oil,",
    "For the pizza dough: 650 ml water,",
    "For the topping: 2 x 400g tins San Marzano tomatoes,",
    "For the topping: 2 large cow’s milk mozzarella balls (grated),",
    "For the topping: 3 tbsp olive oil,",
    "For the topping: 3 tbsp peanut oil,",
    "For the topping: sea salt and freshly ground black pepper,",
    "For the topping: 1 large handful basil leaves."
  ],
  "recipeInstructions": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "text": "1. To make the dough, place the flours, salt, sugar and yeast into a large bowl and stir. Make a well in the centre of the flour and pour in the water, gradually mixing in the flour to form a soft dough."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "text": "2. Tip out and knead on an oiled work surface for about 10 minutes or until the dough is smooth and elastic. Place it into a bowl, cover and leave to rise for 24 hours."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "text": "3. Preheat the oven to its highest temperature. Place a heavy baking tray or pizza stone in the oven and allow it to heat."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "text": "4. Remove the dough from the bowl and knead for a couple of minutes, then divide into four pieces. Allow to rest for five minutes, then stretch each piece over your hands to form a disc about 5mm/¼in thick."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "text": "5. Place the circles of dough onto upturned floured baking trays or a pizza peel if you have one."
    },
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "text": "6. Place the tinned tomatoes into a food processor and blend to a purée. Spoon the tomato purée thinly over the pizzas, scatter over the grated mozzarella, then drizzle over the oils. Season with sea salt and freshly ground black pepper."
